WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.490
We know we need to increase the strength

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.290
of the left ventricle as a starting place.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:08.160
If we do that, that will
allow or actually produce

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.950
and result in an increase
in stroke volume.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.660
So, what does that mean for training?

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.070
Well, fundamentally,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.950
outside of things like exercise technique

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.190
and timing and nutrition
and all that other stuff,

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.550
if we're just talking about
the background physiology,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.880
we have two avenues or areas to push on

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.500
to improve our VO2 max.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.953
We have our stroke volume
and our a-vO2 difference.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:33.840
So, there's a lot of ways

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.940
we go about improving both of them.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.680
I am of the opinion

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.350
that you need to train
across a wide spectrum

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:45.150
of exercise intensities
to optimize both factors.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.610
If you in fact look at
classic training logs

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:48.750
of endurance athletes,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.360
going back to even what we
know about Oscar's training,

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.930
they are typically going to
spend something like 70% or so

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.930
of their time at a low intensity.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.100
What's that mean exactly?

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.350
Mm, depends on the athlete,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.090
but you're probably talking
about something like

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:08.090
between 60 to 80, maybe up to
82% of their heart rate peak.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.840
Most of their time is there.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.580
I'll explain why in a second.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.700
Then, you've got another
additional maybe 20 to 25%

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.860
of your time being spent
at a moderate intensity.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.140
Mm, typically something like, again,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.160
82 to 90 or so percent
of your heart rate peak.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.820
And then, three to maybe 6% of the time

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.140
at the remaining higher heart rate.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.510
So, this is 92, 93% or so plus.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.700
The reason I'm giving you
kind of rough guidelines there

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.600
is every scientific paper
has those zones, if you will,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.130
a little bit differently.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.280
All kinds of different
endurance coaches historically

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.900
have set different landmarks and so,

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.060
there's no exact numbers there.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.640
And so as a very rough guideline,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.720
I think it is very safe to
assume some split like that

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.660
should be highly effective
at improving your VO2 max.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.640
What's that mean in terms of exercises?

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.250
Well, actually it's entirely up to you.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.540
VO2 max is, remember,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.270
dependent upon how many
milliliters of oxygen

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.750
per kilogram of muscle per minute,

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.960
which means the more muscles you utilize,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.580
the higher the VO2 max is.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.400
If you were to go to
get a VO2 max test done,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.830
and let's say you were
not specifically trained

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:17.943
on like a bike,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.530
if you were to get that same
exact test done on a bicycle

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:23.580
versus a treadmill,

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.560
where you're running versus cycling,

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.670
the score on the treadmill

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.180
is going to be about 10% or so
higher than it is on the bike.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.760
And that's simply because
there's a small increase

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.410
in the amount of muscles involved

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.130
when it comes to running versus cycling.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.560
Now, if you are specifically
trained on the bike

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.580
and you cycle a lot,

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.840
that may not actually be the case.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.070
And in fact,

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.070
highly endurance-trained
folks on, cyclists, rather,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.850
will score higher on a VO2 max test

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.090
on the bike than they will on a treadmill.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.440
But that really is now coming down

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.930
to test specificity, efficiency,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.130
like all the things that are,

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.320
that's not what we're
trying to talk about here.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.620
And so, generally, the
more muscles involved,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.050
the more oxygen being utilized,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.040
the higher that VO2 max.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.240
So when it comes to training,

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.550
we want to think about the same thing.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.350
The exercise mode,

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.450
I don't want to say it doesn't matter.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:16.110
It is relevant, but you
have unlimited options.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:20.790
If you want to bike or swim
or cycle or row, that's great.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.010
If you don't like any of
those traditional modalities

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.200
and you want to use something
like an assault bike

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.860
or pull a sled, run
uphill, drag something,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.080
those are also incredibly viable options.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.680
It's not the exercise per se
that determines the adaptation,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.650
it's the application
of the exercise, right?

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.690
The body works and physiology works

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.420
on a principle called the SAID principle,

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.253
S-A-I-D,

00:03:43.253 --> 00:03:47.250
which stands for specific
adaptation to imposed demand.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.180
So you put a demand on a tissue

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.730
to bring in and utilize
oxygen at a high rate,

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.280
it will adapt to that specific demand.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.190
So challenging your muscles continuously

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.890
to bring in and utilize
oxygen at a rapid rate

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.930
is all fundamentally that needs to happen

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.037
for you to improve that.

00:04:05.037 --> 00:04:06.930
And so again, the mode of the exercise

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.190
is not that big of a deal.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.020
If you are new to exercise,

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:13.350
I would generally
recommend you being careful

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.900
of exercises that involve
a lot of eccentric action.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.040
So, jumping and landing,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.800
because you're going to get
really sore really fast.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:20.640
But if not,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.740
feel free to choose
whatever exercise modality

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.270
or a combination of them.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.560
Switch it up a little bit,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.490
Do some cycling, do some running
uphill, jump in the pool.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.573
Really up to you.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.360
The intensity in which you do that

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.253
is more like what I just explained.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:37.950
As I apologized at the
beginning of the program,

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.390
I, earlier in my life,

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:42.450
grossly underappreciated
the cardiovascular system

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:43.560
as a whole,

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.570
and I certainly underappreciated

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.850
the importance of low-intensity exercise.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.310
I'll also be candid with you here.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.540
I am not as fond of zone two exercise

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:55.560
as some other folks are.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.990
I certainly don't think it's
bad. It is good for you.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.820
I just don't think you
need to be that worried

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.920
about what exact zone you're in.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.560
You want to be something
probably in that lower intensity,

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.710
60 to 80-ish percent of your heart rate.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.990
I don't really care where
your millimolars are.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.060
In any of those low intensities,

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.490
you're going to be challenging
the ability to bring in

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.700
and utilize oxygen over
a long period of time.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.710
Look at any amount of research on that.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:20.700
It is very clear.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.830
Steady-state, lower-intensity exercise,

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:26.133
especially over time,
six months to a year,

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.280
is generally going to improve VO2 max,

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.760
probably upwards of five to
10% depending on the person,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.040
the training history and
other contexts like that.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.270
So it's very, very effective

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.430
and something I have absolutely
incorporated more and more

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.710
into both my life personally

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.510
as well as my coaching practice.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.790
So, really important to do that stuff.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.800
On the other end of the equation,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.530
you can do things at an
extremely high intensity

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:50.553
for a short about.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:52.920
Depending on the study
you want to pull here,

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.580
you can see things like
high-intensity intervals.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.660
This could be a combination

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.450
of 30 seconds of maximal exercise,

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.010
resting 30 seconds,

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.850
and repeating that anywhere
between like four and 12 times

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.740
can equally improve VO2 max,

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.523
if not greater and more so than
your steady-state exercise.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:14.520
There's a lot more
context that go into that.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.250
It's not necessarily meaning
high intensity is better.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.920
There are some significant
downsides and concerns

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.530
with only doing high-intensity exercise,

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.170
another thing I've changed my opinion on.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:28.110
And so, I think we want to
use high-intensity exercise.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.510
There's clear benefit there.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.460
It's fundamentally different, though,

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:33.840
than low-intensity exercise.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.270
So, we're challenging a
different part of the system,

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.280
which is why I'm going to argue

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.450
you should be incorporating
both most of the time.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:45.210
It doesn't have to be always
in all of your training,

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.140
but you wouldn't want to leave

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.820
either one of these things entirely off

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.093
if the pure goal here is to maximize VO2.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:53.850
The reason is,

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.490
when you do something
at a higher intensity,

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:00.360
the point of failure in the
tissue becomes different.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.310
So, extending my ability to move

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:05.760
at a lower or moderate intensity
for a long period of time

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.190
is challenging different aspects

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:09.360
than it is when I ask it

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.670
to introduce a tremendous
amount of fatigue.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.560
So, I'm now into anaerobic metabolism

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.080
when I'm going really
hard and really fast.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:21.000
I can't use oxygen, so I'm
building up a ton of byproducts.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.420
pH is being disturbed,
potential damage is happening,

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:25.710
other things are occurring,

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.690
CO2 is getting extremely high.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:31.110
And so, enhancing my
ability to deal with that

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.360
is a similar thing

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.360
in terms of increasing
mitochondria biogenesis.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:37.590
So, more mitochondria,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.350
higher-functioning mitochondria,
larger mitochondria,

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.000
increasing aerobic capacity.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.563
All of these same things occur.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.100
And so, again, I don't want
to make the argument that one,

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.710
higher intensity or low
intensity is better than another.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.720
I think you should do both.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.110
I will make the same argument
for moderate intensity.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.080
While that isn't as specific and precise

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.850
in terms of what it's challenging,

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:01.500
it's reasonable to build some of that

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:02.790
into your equation as well.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:05.880
Another thing you're going to
find commonly in the research

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:08.100
is a longer about of intervals.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.590
This is described in a
lot of different ways.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.240
A good friend of mine

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.600
and an expert in endurance
physiology, Joel Jamieson,

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.050
has talked a lot about
high-intensity continuous training,

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.370
HICT.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:21.600
If you're not familiar with that stuff,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.740
I would encourage you to look it up.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.570
It's very, very effective.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.600
Lots of different things and
tools we can pull out here.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.880
One example would be something like,

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.212
let's go with a classic runner,

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:35.940
who would do something
more like one mile repeats.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.680
So, run a mile as fast as you can.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.270
This is going to take most folks,

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.270
you know, six to eight
or nine minutes or so.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.130
However long it takes
you to run that mile,

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.330
rest that same amount of time.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.730
So, it's a one to one work to rest ratio.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.970
So six minutes of running,
six minutes of rest,

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:52.260
and then you repeat that again

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.740
for a total of two or three
or perhaps four repetitions.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.120
That's a very long workout

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.760
and the average person would
not be able to do that.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.057
But those of you that are not average,

00:09:01.057 --> 00:09:02.700
that are good to high

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.100
to strong performers listening right now,

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.420
that's absolutely within your capabilities

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.580
and in fact, you've
probably done it before.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:10.320
It doesn't have to be that extreme.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.270
You could use shorter durations,

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:13.860
say two minutes, three minutes.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.810
Four minutes is a very, very common one

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:16.890
you'll find in research.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.290
So four minutes of all out exercise,

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:23.520
four minutes of recovery, repeat
it again two to four times.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:26.500
What's critical to
understand here is these work

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.310
when you're actually achieving a maximum

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:30.840
in that time domain.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.090
So, you can't do four minutes at 70%,

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.190
rest for four minutes and do that again.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.690
That's going to burn you some calories

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:39.300
and has other benefits of just
making you feel better today

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:40.770
and some other stuff like that.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:41.760
But in terms of VO2 max,

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.640
it's probably not the most
efficient thing you can do.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.350
So to summarize all of that stuff,

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.920
spend a good amount of
time at a lower intensity.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.840
That's going to drive efficiency,

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.430
a common adaptation there,

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:57.150
since it's going to be the
highest activity you can do

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:00.300
to maximize utilizing fat for fuel.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.940
You're still going to be
burning primarily carbohydrates,

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.530
don't get that confused,

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:06.600
but that's the best way to burn some fat.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.040
So, this is typically associated

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.173
with higher metabolic efficiency,

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.780
getting better at using
fat as a fuel source

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:13.650
and things like that.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.540
It's also easy to recover from.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.040
It's not going to change

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.690
your autonomic nervous system that much,

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:21.810
so you typically don't see big drops

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.420
in HRV scores.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.420
We don't really see as much over-training

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.340
or non-functional overreaching,

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.230
elevations in respiratory heart rate,

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.570
other signs of hunger or fatigue,

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:34.403
not wanting to train.

00:10:34.403 --> 00:10:36.600
Things like that doesn't really happen

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.370
when we spend time at lower intensities.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.470
Higher intensities are phenomenal.

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:43.050
Really, really, really time efficient,

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.480
but they've got consequences as well.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.810
They're going to be entirely
or mostly anaerobic,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:49.920
which is okay too

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.530
'cause you'll still use the
aerobic side of the equation

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:53.760
to recover from that.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:54.783
So, super important.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.530
But there's a price to be paid there.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:00.120
People can run into problems

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.520
and you're more likely to see issues

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:05.190
with those metrics I just described.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.020
If you're doing too much
intensity too often,

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:09.610
especially if you're combining this

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:13.260
with a normal stressful life.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:14.940
So you're doing this kind of exercise,

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.430
then you're going right
back into your day job.

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:18.600
You're having difficult meetings,

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.100
even if they're exciting
and happy meetings,

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:21.450
you're thinking hard, you're working,

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:22.283
you're getting back to forth,

00:11:22.283 --> 00:11:23.640
and you're in a kind of a long,

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.950
high-stress environment all day.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.990
Really, really challenging on the system

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.000
to be in that high of
a stress at all times.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.830
So other ways you can mitigate that,

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:33.840
we can talk about those
in future episodes.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.070
But just wanted to say,

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.580
while high intensity exercise
is very time efficient,

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.520
it's not necessarily a free pass either.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.490
Low intensity, not a free pass either.

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.370
It's going to leave things on
the table that you're missing.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:48.630
So to round all that up, again,

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:51.420
I would recommend a
combination of lower intensity,

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:53.610
moderate intensity, and
high intensity training.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:55.590
The mode of the exercise

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.750
in terms of what you choose,

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.690
bicycle, kettlebells, circuit training,

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:01.620
it's entirely up to you.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.960
Spin class, whatever you'd like to do.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.170
Frequency can be as high
or as low as you'd like.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.270
There are plenty of studies showing

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.220
kind of the higher intensity stuff

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:14.730
done two to three times per
week can improve VO2 max.

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.370
But you can also do the lower
intensity stuff every day

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:18.203
or a combination.

00:12:18.203 --> 00:12:21.210
So really, you can modify
this based on your lifestyle

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:22.260
and what's going on.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:23.490
And finally, rest intervals,

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:24.720
they're not incredibly applicable here.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:26.160
In fact, we've already baked them in.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.110
If you're not doing intervals,

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:29.040
there is no rest interval.

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:30.870
If you are, we typically look

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:33.600
for something like a
one-to-one work to rest ratio.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:36.510
But you're welcome to do
two to one, one to two,

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.090
or any combination of that.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:40.500
If you train appropriately,

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.270
and of course you've got
all the other factors

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:43.590
like your nutrition and sleep

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.570
and stress management under control,

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.900
it's not unrealistic to
expect a 30 to 50% improvement

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.810
in VO2 max after six to 12 months.

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:56.100
You'll find plenty of studies
that land in that ballpark.

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.040
The rate of increase obviously goes down

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.050
as you become more and more trained.

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:03.240
Now, candidly, you don't have the ability

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:04.890
to improve your VO2 max probably as much

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.470
as you do something like your strength,

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.450
but you can improve it
significantly nonetheless.

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.270
So, you will find plenty of studies

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.790
showing even a 10 to 20% increase

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:17.880
in highly-trained
individuals after a year.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:18.810
In untrained folks,

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:20.130
that probably takes about half that time,

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:23.370
so 10 to 20% improvement in
four to six months or so.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:25.320
So if you know where you're at right now,

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.200
you train appropriately,
fairly consistently,

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:29.970
again, those are reasonable
numbers to expect

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:32.610
after a half a year or so of training.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.050
And as we understand it,

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.060
the biggest limiting factor at this point

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.010
is probably the time needed

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.110
to fill the ventricles back up with blood.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.110
[music]

